this kind of analysis  on how  not just genomic workflows but pretty  much any any  medical workflow  performs on hardware  we  we wrote a tool to do this these types  of graphs and this type of analysis and  it's released open source  um it's called workflow profiler  um and what we've noticed is that as you  can see over here  the um  the lines  this is cpu utilization most of the time  most of this most of the cores are not  even used right there's there are huge  spaces here where  we're not using cpus  um  likewise  um  i can show you with memory  modern servers have hundreds of  gigabytes of ram right and  and many of these workloads are hardly  using any memory except maybe during  some some peak times  so  there's a lot of room for improvement  there's a lot of room to to write  software that actually uses the features  that are in the hardware  that for the most part in all the sort  of bioinformatics fields that we're  serving are not being used  all right so let me get back to the the  variant calling problem  we we profiled that workload that was  taking 100 hours and we realized that  the  the heart of the whole problem was this  kernel  so this is a very complicated piece of  software had  tens of thousands of lines of code part  of a bigger program program that  hundreds of thousands of lines of code  but 70 of the runtime was just  these lines of code  they were written in java they weren't  taking advantage of  of um  data locality  uh or any of the features of  we we decoded what this was trying to do  we figured out this is trying to do a  hidden markov model calculation  we figured out what the dependencies  were  and i'm not going to go through the  details of it  but it was fairly elegant  in that  we really have we first computed one  value  in this matrix and then we have a  dependency here  where there are other two cells in this  matrix that depend on that first value  and then there are  uh three other cells that depend on  those two values and then and so on  and there's a progression uh when you  run this  um  the way this was being run is that  each cell was being calculated  independently so you first you calculate  one cell then you go to the next cell  and you go to the next cell  each cell it takes a certain number of  steps  what we did is we rewrote the pro we  wrote those few lines of code in a  vectorized manner  that means is that  instead of treating each cell as my  variable i treat each  anti-diagonal as a variable that's  called a vector  so i can do this calculation  one chunk at a time  so for example in in terms of  calculating those  values over there i can i can calculate  it in five steps  instead of doing it in what would that  be um 5 4 9 12 14 steps as you can see  as this gets wider and wider  it saves me a huge amount of time  um that was actually a fairly easy  fix  but had huge impact it meant that  instead of taking 100 hours to do this  this  computation  it was taking only  30 hours so  huge difference um  the the results were actually kind of  spectacular we got 970x speed up  compared to the original version so once  you start using vectorization  the optimizations that you can do in c  plus plus plus  multi-core we can actually be able to  prove that we can run a thousand times  faster than java  uh you know if you look at the downloads  of gdk over time um the version that we  improved actually became  the  the the most downloaded version ever of  gtk and jtk this is this uh um you know  genomics pipeline  so it had a you know vast impact on  industry  um that's why one is one specific  example that i have now i'm just going  to give a very brief overview of other  things that we're doing  um  there are a vast num many many other  types of software out there in the  bioinformatics world  the left is  more on genomics and the right is more  on molecular dynamics we have teams that  are working on all these codes and  optimizing all of these  i don't know if you're familiar with any  of these but uh  the there we've done some  uh pretty um  pretty good improvements in many of  these cases  um we also partner with a lot of  companies and startups  uh we have a partnership with a company  called asd uh where we also  used adx  vectorization to get  um  speed up so we saw 400 improvement in  their uh operations uh we also have a  collaboration with scripps um in which  we saw  for also forex improvement for but for a  different reason this was about about  faster io using ssds  aspara is a company that does um fast  communication over network which is  actually very important these data sets  are huge  so ftp doesn't work you have to use  something  faster than that we've collaborated with  them to do that  sanger institute is a very famous  institute in the uk  we work with them in terms of storage  they're using a luster file system that  we've built for them  finally  in terms of appliances this is actually  a very key thing is you know taking us  an intel server  and being able to customize it  for life sciences applications is  something we did with dell  and  with a very nice story we were able to  cut down  the uh the delay for this was done with  dell in another institute in arizona  t-gen  uh and they were using this for for kids  that had uh  cancer  um and uh we were able to to bring down  the uh the time to do the rna-seq which  is a different type of sequencing not  dna with rna down from seven days to  under a day  which is kind of critical for some of  these  um you know very acute uh patients  likewise we have a number of  collaborations with a bunch of  appliances  uh to do this type of optimization work  in life sciences um  if any of you wants to build an  appliance you're you're welcome and  you get help from us and  that's it  that's all i have i think  as i mentioned before really our our  goal and this is being also stated by  our um  the head of our organization uh as in  servers as well as our ceo  is that we want to make personalized  medicine mainstream for 2020.  we do have a url  on the top that gives some examples of  the work we've done with industry  that's my email if you want to contact  me
 

